<file path="frontend_overview">
---

## The Mental Model First

This app has **two completely separate systems** that just happen to live in the same codebase:

1. **Tasks** — standalone to-dos (Today, Upcoming, Labels pages)
2. **Projects** — AI-generated multi-phase plans with their own tasks inside them

They barely talk to each other. A project's tasks are stored inside the project object. The standalone tasks in TaskContext are a different thing entirely.

---

## How "Projects" Works End to End

### The types — understand these first before anything else

There are two worlds of types and a conversion function between them.

**The "creation world" (`src/types/creation.ts`)** — temporary, only exists during the modal flow:
- `ProjectDraft` — what the user typed (goal, description, date, 3 clarifying answers)
- `GeneratedPhase` / `GeneratedTask` — what the AI spits back (simplified, no `done`, no `dueDate`)

**The "app world" (`src/types/project.ts` + `src/types/task.ts`)** — permanent, what gets stored:
- `Project` — the full thing with phases, insights, attachments, coaching notes
- `Phase` — one step of the project with status, progress, and tasks
- `PhaseTask` — a task that belongs to a phase (extends the base `Task` type)

**The conversion function** is `draftToProject()` in `src/lib/utils/project-utils.ts`. It takes a `ProjectDraft` + `GeneratedPhase[]` and produces a real `Project`. First phase becomes `"in-progress"`, rest become `"upcoming"`, all tasks start as `done: false`. This is the exact moment the temporary creation data becomes a permanent project.

---

## The Fake Database

`src/lib/mock/projects.ts` is your entire database right now. It's just two JS arrays:

- `STATIC_PROJECTS` — the two hand-written projects (Internship Search, Japan Trip) that always exist
- `dynamicProjects` — an empty array that gets projects pushed into it during the app session

It exports three functions: `getProjectById`, `getAllProjects`, `addProject`. Those are the only three things the API routes ever call. When Supabase comes, you delete this file and swap those three functions for DB queries. Nothing else changes.

**Critical thing to know:** `dynamicProjects` is just a variable in memory. Every time the server restarts it goes back to `[]`. New projects you create only survive until you refresh.

---

## The API Routes

`src/app/api/` — these run **on the server only**, never in the browser. They're the wall between the browser and the mock store.

**`POST /api/projects`** — called when the modal finishes. Receives the draft, generates a `mock-<timestamp>` ID, returns `{ id }`. It does NOT save the draft content anywhere. Just hands back an ID.

**`GET /api/projects/[id]`** — called when any project page loads. Calls `getProjectById(id)`. Returns the project or 404. Right now this 404s for newly created projects because of the navigation bug — the page hits this endpoint before the project has been saved.

**`PUT /api/projects/[id]`** — called after generation is complete. Receives the full generated `Project` object, stamps the URL's ID onto it, calls `addProject()`. This is the moment a new project actually becomes findable.

**`GET /api/projects`** — returns all projects, used by the sidebar.

---

## The Client API Layer

`src/lib/api/projects.ts` — these run **in the browser**. They're just `fetch()` wrappers with proper TypeScript types. There's no logic here, just HTTP calls to the routes above.

The one exception is `generateProjectPlan()` — it doesn't call an API route yet. It waits 3 seconds (so the animation plays) then returns hardcoded `MOCK_PHASES`. The TODO comment says swap this for a real call to `/api/ai` which would hit Anthropic. That's where the actual AI integration goes.

---

## The Creation Flow Step by Step

1. User opens modal → `useCreateProject` hook in `UseCreateProject.tsx` just controls open/closed state. Dead simple.

2. Modal renders via `CreateProjectModal.tsx` — this file is purely visual. The progress bar, the heading, the close button. It imports `useProjectCreation` for all actual logic.

3. `useProjectCreation` in `src/lib/utils/project-creation.ts` is the brain. It holds the current step, the accumulating draft, and the submit logic. `handleOutcomeNext` just saves step 1 data and moves to step 2. `handleClarifyNext` is where it actually does work: POSTs to the API, gets the ID back, calls `onClose()`, then navigates.

4. **The navigation line is the bug.** It currently does `router.push('/projects/${id}?status=generating')` which sends you to the permanent project page at `/projects/[id]`. That page immediately tries to `GET /api/projects/mock-xxx` — but nothing has been saved there yet. Should be `router.push('/projects/new?status=generating&id=${id}')`.

5. `src/app/projects/new/page.tsx` is the generating page. It reads `?status=generating` and `?id=` from the URL. The `useEffect` runs once on mount: calls `generateProjectPlan` (3 second fake wait), calls `draftToProject` to convert the result, calls `saveProject` (the PUT route), then `router.replace` to the permanent page. While all this happens it shows `<StepGenerating />` which is just the animation component.

6. `src/app/projects/[id]/page.tsx` is the permanent page. On mount it calls `getProject(id)` and renders whatever comes back. Four tabs: Overview, Tasks, Insights, Files.

---

## The Tasks System (separate thing)

`src/context/TaskContext.tsx` — React Context that holds all standalone tasks for the whole app. Fetches from `/api/tasks` once when the app loads, keeps a local copy. Today/Upcoming/Labels all read from this same context so they stay in sync. The optimistic update pattern means: flip the state immediately in the browser, then call the API. If the API fails, roll it back.

This has nothing to do with project phase tasks. Project tasks live inside `project.phases[n].tasks` and are fetched as part of the project. They're a separate thing.

---

## The lib/utils Files

These are pure helper functions — no fetch, no React, just data transformations:

- `project-utils.ts` — just `draftToProject()`, the conversion function
- `task-display-utils.ts` — given a task's data, what tag color/label should show in the UI
- `date-utils.ts` — is this date today? tomorrow? overdue?
- `insight-icon.tsx` — maps string keys like `"trending-up"` to actual Lucide icon components. Icons are stored as strings so they can be serialized to JSON/Supabase, then swapped for real components at render time
- `label-utils.ts` — helpers for the Labels page, grouping tasks by tag
</file>

